var e={id:`group7`,objectives:[`Describe the trends in volatility and colour of Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂ using van der Waals forces`,`Explain the relative reactivity of halogens as oxidising agents using E° values`,`Write equations for displacement reactions of halogens with halide solutions`,`Explain the relative thermal stabilities of HCl, HBr and HI in terms of bond energies`,`Describe the reactions of halide ions with AgNO₃ and subsequent ammonia tests`,`Describe and explain the reactions of halide ions with concentrated H₂SO₄`,`Write equations for the disproportionation of Cl₂ with cold and hot NaOH`,`Explain the industrial importance (water purification, bleach, PVC) and environmental significance (CFCs, ozone depletion) of halogens and their compounds`],chapters:[{id:`g71`,title:`The Halogens: Cl₂, Br₂ & I₂`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Group VII contains fluorine (F), chlorine (Cl), bromine (Br), iodine (I) and astatine (At). The ZIMSEC syllabus focuses on Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂. These are called the HALOGENS (Greek: "salt-formers") because they react with metals to form salts. All have the outer electron configuration ns²np⁵ — they need just ONE more electron to achieve a noble gas configuration, which is why they are powerful oxidising agents.`},{type:`h`,text:`Key features`},{type:`ul`,items:[`All exist as diatomic molecules: F₂, Cl₂, Br₂, I₂.`,`All are coloured and toxic.`,`They are the most electronegative elements (F is the most electronegative of all).`,`Reactivity DECREASES down the group (opposite to Groups I and II).`,`They form −1 ions (halide ions: Cl⁻, Br⁻, I⁻).`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Group VII: F, Cl, Br, I — configuration ns²np⁵.`,`Exist as X₂ molecules. Reactivity decreases down.`,`Powerful oxidising agents: X₂ + 2e⁻ → 2X⁻.`]}]},{id:`g72`,title:`Colour, Volatility & Van der Waals Forces`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`The halogens show a clear trend in physical appearance: colour deepens and volatility decreases going down the group.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`GROUP VII PHYSICAL PROPERTIES`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Halogen`,`Colour`,`State`,`B.P./°C`,`E°/V`],rows:[[`F₂`,`pale yellow`,`gas`,`−188`,`+2.87`],[`Cl₂`,`yellow-green`,`gas`,`−34`,`+1.36`],[`Br₂`,`red-brown`,`liquid`,`+59`,`+1.07`],[`I₂`,`grey-black*`,`solid`,`+184`,`+0.54`]],rowColors:[`amber`,`green`,`pink`,`primary`]},{type:`note`,text:`*I₂ gives purple vapour when heated; brown in solution`},{type:`note`,text:`↓ group: ↑ size → ↑ London forces → ↑ b.p. → ↓ volatility`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Physical properties of halogens`},{type:`h`,text:`Colour trend`},{type:`ul`,items:[`F₂: pale yellow gas.`,`Cl₂: yellow-green gas (pungent, choking smell).`,`Br₂: red-brown volatile liquid (fuming at room temperature).`,`I₂: grey-black solid with a shiny lustre. Sublimes to give PURPLE vapour. Brown when dissolved in organic solvents; brown-yellow in aqueous solution.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why volatility decreases`},{type:`ul`,items:[`All halogens are simple molecular (X₂) with non-polar covalent bonds.`,`The intermolecular forces are VAN DER WAALS (London dispersion) forces only.`,`Going down: molecular size and number of electrons INCREASE → London forces get STRONGER.`,`Stronger intermolecular forces → higher boiling point → less volatile.`,`F₂ (gas) → Cl₂ (gas) → Br₂ (liquid) → I₂ (solid) at room temperature.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Volatility = ease of evaporation`,body:`High volatility means low boiling point. F₂ is the most volatile halogen (b.p. −188 °C) because it has the fewest electrons and weakest London forces. I₂ is the least volatile (b.p. +184 °C) with the strongest London forces.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Colour deepens: pale yellow → yellow-green → red-brown → grey-black.`,`Volatility decreases (b.p. increases): more electrons → stronger London forces.`,`All are simple molecular — only London forces between X₂ molecules.`]}]},{id:`g73`,title:`Reactivity & E° Values`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Halogens are oxidising agents — they accept electrons to form halide ions: X₂ + 2e⁻ → 2X⁻. Reactivity as oxidising agents DECREASES down the group: F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`OXIDISING POWER · E° VALUES`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Halogen`,`E°/V`,`Strength`],colWidths:[70,80,140],rows:[[`F₂`,`+2.87 V`,`strongest oxidiser`],[`Cl₂`,`+1.36 V`,`very strong`],[`Br₂`,`+1.07 V`,`moderate`],[`I₂`,`+0.54 V`,`weakest`]],rowColors:[`amber`,`green`,`pink`,`primary`]},{type:`note`,text:`higher E° → stronger oxidising agent → more reactive`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`oxidising power DECREASES down the group`}]},caption:`E° values decrease down Group VII`},{type:`h`,text:`Why reactivity decreases`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Going down: atomic radius INCREASES. The incoming electron is added to a shell further from the nucleus.`,`More inner shells provide greater SHIELDING of the nuclear charge.`,`The effective nuclear attraction for the incoming electron is WEAKER.`,`Therefore it becomes harder for the halogen to gain an electron → lower E° → weaker oxidising agent.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Displacement reactions`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`HALOGEN DISPLACEMENT REACTIONS`,subtitle:`a more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[``,`Cl⁻(aq)`,`Br⁻(aq)`,`I⁻(aq)`],rows:[[`Cl₂`,`—`,`✓ brown`,`✓ brown→purple`],[`Br₂`,`✗`,`—`,`✓ brown→purple`],[`I₂`,`✗`,`✗`,`—`]]},{type:`equation`,text:`Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂`,big:!0},{type:`equation`,text:`Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂`},{type:`note`,text:`Cl₂ is the strongest oxidising agent; I₂ is the weakest`}]},caption:`A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halide from solution`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂. Cl₂ oxidises Br⁻ to Br₂. Solution turns ORANGE/BROWN.`,`Cl₂ + 2KI → 2KCl + I₂. Cl₂ oxidises I⁻ to I₂. Solution turns BROWN (then dark/purple with starch).`,`Br₂ + 2KI → 2KBr + I₂. Br₂ oxidises I⁻. Solution turns brown/black.`,`I₂ cannot displace Cl⁻ or Br⁻ — it is the weakest oxidising agent.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Using E° to predict`,body:`E°cell = E°(oxidiser) − E°(reducer). Cl₂/Cl⁻ = +1.36 V and Br₂/Br⁻ = +1.07 V. E°cell = 1.36 − 1.07 = +0.29 V > 0 → Cl₂ WILL oxidise Br⁻. But Br₂ cannot oxidise Cl⁻ because E°cell would be negative.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Reactivity as oxidising agents: F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂.`,`Explained by increasing atomic radius and shielding down the group.`,`A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halide.`]}]},{id:`g74`,title:`Hydrogen Halides & Thermal Stability`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`All halogens react with hydrogen to form hydrogen halides (HX). The vigour of the reaction decreases down the group, and the thermal stability of the product also decreases.`},{type:`h`,text:`Reactions with hydrogen`},{type:`ul`,items:[`H₂ + F₂ → 2HF. EXPLOSIVE, even in the dark and at low temperatures.`,`H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. Explosive in UV light or at high temperatures. Slow in the dark.`,`H₂ + Br₂ → 2HBr. Requires heating. Less vigorous.`,`H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI. Slow, requires a catalyst (Pt), and the reaction is REVERSIBLE.`]},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`HYDROGEN HALIDE THERMAL STABILITY`,blocks:[{type:`bars`,yMax:100,items:[{label:`HF`,value:95,valueLabel:`95`,color:`primary`,sublabel:`very stable`},{label:`HCl`,value:75,valueLabel:`75`,color:`primary`,sublabel:`stable`},{label:`HBr`,value:45,valueLabel:`45`,color:`primary`,sublabel:`decomp.`},{label:`HI`,value:20,valueLabel:`20`,color:`primary`,sublabel:`easy decomp.`}]},{type:`note`,text:`Stability DECREASES down the group`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`longer H–X bond → weaker → easier to break → less stable`}]},caption:`Thermal stability of hydrogen halides decreases down the group`},{type:`h`,text:`Why stability decreases`},{type:`ul`,items:[`H–F bond is very short and strong (568 kJ mol⁻¹) → HF is extremely stable.`,`H–Cl: 432 kJ mol⁻¹. H–Br: 366 kJ mol⁻¹. H–I: 298 kJ mol⁻¹.`,`Going down: the H–X bond gets LONGER and WEAKER because the halogen atom is larger.`,`Weaker bonds are easier to break → HI decomposes at relatively low temperatures; HF is essentially unbreakable thermally.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Bond strength explains both trends`,body:`The decreasing bond energy H–F > H–Cl > H–Br > H–I explains both (1) the decreasing vigour of the H₂ + X₂ reaction (less energy released as the bond formed is weaker) and (2) the decreasing thermal stability (weaker bonds break more easily).`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Vigour of H₂ + X₂ decreases: F₂ explosive → I₂ slow/reversible.`,`Thermal stability: HF > HCl > HBr > HI.`,`Explained by decreasing H–X bond energy down the group.`]}]},{id:`g75`,title:`Testing for Halide Ions: Ag⁺ & NH₃`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`The standard test for halide ions uses silver nitrate solution followed by ammonia. Each halide gives a different coloured precipitate with different solubility in ammonia.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`TESTING FOR HALIDE IONS · Ag⁺/NH₃`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Halide`,`+ AgNO₃`,`+ dil NH₃`,`+ conc NH₃`],rows:[[`Cl⁻`,`white ppt`,`dissolves`,`dissolves`],[`Br⁻`,`cream ppt`,`insoluble`,`dissolves`],[`I⁻`,`yellow ppt`,`insoluble`,`insoluble`]]},{type:`panel`,title:`PROCEDURE`,body:[`Add dilute HNO₃ first (removes CO₃²⁻/SO₃²⁻)`,`then add AgNO₃ → precipitate forms`,`then test with NH₃ to distinguish the halides`],color:`primary`}]},caption:`The Ag⁺/NH₃ test distinguishes Cl⁻, Br⁻ and I⁻`},{type:`h`,text:`Procedure`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Step 1: Acidify the solution with DILUTE NITRIC ACID (HNO₃). This removes interfering ions like CO₃²⁻ and SO₃²⁻ that would also give precipitates with Ag⁺.`,`Step 2: Add silver nitrate solution, AgNO₃(aq).`,`Step 3: Observe the precipitate colour: white (Cl⁻), cream (Br⁻), yellow (I⁻).`,`Step 4: Add dilute ammonia (NH₃) to confirm: AgCl dissolves (soluble), AgBr does not dissolve.`,`Step 5: Add concentrated ammonia: AgBr now dissolves, but AgI still does not.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Equations`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) — white precipitate.`,`Ag⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq) → AgBr(s) — cream precipitate.`,`Ag⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq) → AgI(s) — yellow precipitate.`,`AgCl dissolves in dilute NH₃: AgCl + 2NH₃ → [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ + Cl⁻ (diamminesilver(I) complex).`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Why HNO₃ not HCl?`,body:`You must use nitric acid, NOT hydrochloric acid. HCl would introduce Cl⁻ ions, giving a false positive for chloride. HNO₃ does not contain any halide ions.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Acidify with HNO₃, then add AgNO₃.`,`White = Cl⁻ (dissolves in dil. NH₃). Cream = Br⁻ (dissolves in conc. NH₃). Yellow = I⁻ (insoluble in NH₃).`,`Use HNO₃, never HCl, to acidify.`]}]},{id:`g76`,title:`Halide Ions + Concentrated H₂SO₄`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`When solid sodium halides are treated with concentrated sulphuric acid, the products depend on whether the hydrogen halide (HX) is a strong enough reducing agent to reduce H₂SO₄.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`HALIDE IONS + CONC. H₂SO₄`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Halide`,`Products`,`Observations`],colWidths:[50,130,110],rows:[[`Cl⁻`,`HCl only`,`white fumes`],[`Br⁻`,`HBr → Br₂ + SO₂`,`brown fumes`],[`I⁻`,`HI → I₂ + H₂S`,`purple, rotten egg`]]},{type:`note`,text:`Why the difference?`,emphasis:!0},{type:`panel`,body:[`HCl: not strong enough to reduce H₂SO₄`,`HBr: reduces H₂SO₄ to SO₂ (Br⁻ better reducer)`,`HI: reduces H₂SO₄ all the way to H₂S (I⁻ strongest reducer)`],color:`primary`}]},caption:`Products depend on reducing power of the halide`},{type:`h`,text:`NaCl + conc. H₂SO₄`},{type:`ul`,items:[`NaCl + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HCl. White fumes of HCl.`,`HCl is NOT a strong enough reducing agent to reduce H₂SO₄ further.`,`This is a simple acid-base reaction (not redox). H₂SO₄ acts as an acid, donating H⁺ to Cl⁻.`]},{type:`h`,text:`NaBr + conc. H₂SO₄`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Initially: NaBr + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HBr (acid-base, steamy fumes).`,`But HBr IS a reducing agent. It reduces H₂SO₄: 2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O.`,`Observations: brown/orange fumes of Br₂, pungent SO₂ gas.`,`Br⁻ is oxidised from −1 to 0; S is reduced from +6 to +4.`]},{type:`h`,text:`NaI + conc. H₂SO₄`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Initially: NaI + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HI.`,`HI is a VERY strong reducing agent. It reduces H₂SO₄ all the way to H₂S:`,`8HI + H₂SO₄ → 4I₂ + H₂S + 4H₂O.`,`Observations: purple/black I₂ solid/vapour, rotten egg smell of H₂S.`,`I⁻ is oxidised from −1 to 0; S is reduced from +6 to −2 (a 8-electron change!).`,`Also produces SO₂ and S as intermediate reduction products.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Reducing power increases`,body:`Cl⁻: no reduction of H₂SO₄. Br⁻: reduces to SO₂ (S: +6→+4). I⁻: reduces to H₂S (S: +6→−2). This is because the reducing power of X⁻ increases down the group — larger ions hold their outer electrons less tightly and lose them more easily.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`NaCl: HCl only (white fumes, no redox).`,`NaBr: HBr then Br₂ + SO₂ (brown fumes).`,`NaI: HI then I₂ + H₂S (purple/black, rotten egg smell).`,`Reducing power: I⁻ > Br⁻ > Cl⁻.`]}]},{id:`g77`,title:`Disproportionation of Cl₂ with NaOH`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Chlorine reacts with sodium hydroxide in a DISPROPORTIONATION reaction — the same element (Cl) is simultaneously oxidised AND reduced. The products depend on the temperature.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`Cl₂ + NaOH · DISPROPORTIONATION`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`COLD DILUTE NaOH`,body:[`Cl₂ + 2NaOH →`,`NaCl + NaClO + H₂O`,`Cl: 0 → −1 and +1`],color:`cyan`},right:{title:`HOT CONC. NaOH`,body:[`3Cl₂ + 6NaOH →`,`5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O`,`Cl: 0 → −1 and +5`],color:`pink`}},{type:`note`,text:`DISPROPORTIONATION`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`= simultaneous oxidation AND reduction of the SAME element`},{type:`note`,text:`Cold: Cl₂ → Cl⁻(−1) + ClO⁻(+1) — bleach!`},{type:`note`,text:`Hot: Cl₂ → Cl⁻(−1) + ClO₃⁻(+5) — chlorate`}]},caption:`Cold vs hot NaOH gives different products`},{type:`h`,text:`Cold dilute NaOH`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O.`,`Chlorine (oxidation state 0) → Cl⁻ (−1, reduced) and ClO⁻ (+1, oxidised).`,`NaClO is sodium chlorate(I) = bleach (sodium hypochlorite).`,`This reaction is the basis of household bleach manufacture.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Hot concentrated NaOH`},{type:`ul`,items:[`3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O.`,`Chlorine (0) → Cl⁻ (−1, reduced) and ClO₃⁻ (+5, oxidised).`,`NaClO₃ is sodium chlorate(V) — used as a weedkiller.`,`The higher temperature allows further oxidation of Cl to the +5 state.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Cl₂ in cold water`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO (or H⁺ + Cl⁻ + HClO).`,`Also a disproportionation: Cl₂ (0) → Cl⁻ (−1) + HClO (+1).`,`HClO (chloric(I) acid) is the active disinfecting agent in chlorinated water.`,`This equilibrium explains why chlorine both acidifies water AND acts as a bleach/disinfectant.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Disproportionation`,body:`A reaction in which the SAME element is simultaneously OXIDISED and REDUCED. Cl₂ → Cl⁻ (reduction, −1) + ClO⁻ (oxidation, +1). The element goes to both a higher AND lower oxidation state.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Cold NaOH: Cl₂ → NaCl + NaClO (bleach). Cl: 0 → −1 and +1.`,`Hot NaOH: Cl₂ → NaCl + NaClO₃. Cl: 0 → −1 and +5.`,`Cold water: Cl₂ → HCl + HClO (disinfectant).`,`All are disproportionation reactions.`]}]},{id:`g78`,title:`Industrial Importance & Environmental Significance`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`The halogens and their compounds have enormous industrial importance but also raise significant environmental concerns.`},{type:`h`,text:`Uses of chlorine and its compounds`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Water purification: Cl₂ or NaClO kills bacteria by oxidation. Used in drinking water and swimming pools.`,`Bleach manufacture: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO.`,`PVC production: Cl₂ is used to make chloroethene (vinyl chloride), which is polymerised to PVC — a major construction and packaging plastic.`,`Halogenated hydrocarbons: used as solvents (e.g. dichloromethane), refrigerants, and anaesthetics.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Environmental concerns`},{type:`ul`,items:[`CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons): formerly used as refrigerants and aerosol propellants. Extremely stable → persist in the atmosphere → reach the ozone layer.`,`In the stratosphere, UV light breaks C–Cl bonds: CFCl₃ → CFCl₂ + Cl•. The chlorine radical catalytically destroys ozone: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂, then ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂. One Cl• can destroy thousands of O₃ molecules.`,`HFCs and HCFCs are now used as replacements — they break down in the lower atmosphere before reaching the ozone layer.`,`Chlorinated solvents: can contaminate groundwater. Many are now restricted or banned.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Uses of bromine and iodine`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Br₂: fire retardants, photographic chemicals, pharmaceuticals.`,`I₂: antiseptic (tincture of iodine), iodised salt (prevents goitre/thyroid deficiency), medical contrast agents.`,`AgBr: used in traditional photographic film (decomposes in light to form Ag metal).`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Cl₂: water purification, bleach, PVC.`,`CFCs destroy ozone layer — now banned under Montreal Protocol.`,`I₂: antiseptic, iodised salt.`]}]},{id:`g79`,title:`Halogen Oxidation States & Thiosulphate`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Halogens typically have oxidation state −1 in halides, but chlorine can also show +1 (in ClO⁻), +5 (in ClO₃⁻) and +7 (in ClO₄⁻). The exam may also involve the halogen–thiosulphate reaction used in iodometric titrations.`},{type:`h`,text:`Oxidation states of chlorine`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Cl₂: 0 (elemental).`,`Cl⁻: −1 (in HCl, NaCl).`,`ClO⁻ (hypochlorite): +1 (in bleach, NaClO).`,`ClO₃⁻ (chlorate): +5 (in NaClO₃).`,`ClO₄⁻ (perchlorate): +7.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Reaction of halogens with Na₂S₂O₃`},{type:`ul`,items:[`I₂ + 2Na₂S₂O₃ → 2NaI + Na₂S₄O₆ (iodine–thiosulphate titration).`,`I₂ is reduced (0 → −1); S₂O₃²⁻ is oxidised (+2 → +2.5 average in S₄O₆²⁻).`,`This is the basis of iodometric analysis — widely used in quantitative chemistry.`,`Starch indicator: turns blue-black with I₂; colourless at endpoint when all I₂ consumed.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Change in oxidation state for Cl₂ + NaOH`},{type:`p`,text:`In cold NaOH: Cl goes from 0 to both −1 (Cl⁻) and +1 (ClO⁻). In hot NaOH: from 0 to −1 (Cl⁻) and +5 (ClO₃⁻). These changes are used to calculate the mole ratios in the equations.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Cl can be −1, 0, +1, +5, +7.`,`I₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻ (iodometric titration).`,`Disproportionation: 0 → −1 and +1 (cold) or +5 (hot).`]}]},{id:`g710`,title:`Halide Ions as Reducing Agents`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`While halogens are OXIDISING agents, halide ions (X⁻) are REDUCING agents. Their reducing power INCREASES down the group: I⁻ > Br⁻ > Cl⁻ > F⁻.`},{type:`h`,text:`Why reducing power increases`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Going down: ionic radius INCREASES. The outer electron in the halide ion is further from the nucleus and more shielded.`,`It is easier to REMOVE this electron (lower ionisation energy for X⁻ → X + e⁻).`,`Therefore I⁻ is the most easily oxidised halide → strongest reducing agent.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Evidence from conc. H₂SO₄ reactions`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Cl⁻: cannot reduce H₂SO₄ at all → only HCl produced.`,`Br⁻: reduces H₂SO₄ to SO₂ (+6 → +4) → Br₂ produced.`,`I⁻: reduces H₂SO₄ to H₂S (+6 → −2) → I₂ produced.`,`This dramatic difference is direct evidence for increasing reducing power down the group.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Don't confuse the two trends`,body:`Halogens (X₂) are oxidising agents — strength DECREASES down. Halide ions (X⁻) are reducing agents — strength INCREASES down. These are opposite trends for the same reason: electron affinity/IE of the species involved.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Reducing power of X⁻ increases: F⁻ < Cl⁻ < Br⁻ < I⁻.`,`Evidence: reactions with conc. H₂SO₄ show I⁻ is strongest reducer.`,`Opposite trend to halogen oxidising power.`]}]},{id:`g711`,title:`Exam Technique & Common Pitfalls`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Group VII questions combine trend explanations, testing procedures, equations, and redox analysis. Here are the key patterns.`},{type:`h`,text:`Must-know equations`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂ (displacement).`,`Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O (cold — bleach).`,`3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O (hot).`,`Ag⁺ + X⁻ → AgX (precipitate test).`,`NaCl + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HCl.`,`2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O.`,`8HI + H₂SO₄ → 4I₂ + H₂S + 4H₂O.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Common mistakes`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Saying "reactivity increases down Group VII" — WRONG. Oxidising power (reactivity of X₂) DECREASES. Reducing power (of X⁻) INCREASES.`,`Using HCl to acidify in the halide test — use HNO₃ only.`,`Forgetting to state temperature for Cl₂ + NaOH reactions (cold → ClO⁻; hot → ClO₃⁻).`,`Writing the wrong products for NaI + conc. H₂SO₄ — there are MULTIPLE products (I₂, H₂S, S, SO₂) because I⁻ is such a strong reducer.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Colour observations to state in exams`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Cl₂ displaces Br⁻: solution turns ORANGE/BROWN.`,`Cl₂ displaces I⁻: solution turns BROWN (dark) or add starch for BLUE-BLACK.`,`AgCl: WHITE ppt. AgBr: CREAM ppt. AgI: YELLOW ppt.`,`Br₂ fumes: brown/orange. I₂ vapour: purple.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Oxidising power decreases down (X₂); reducing power increases down (X⁻).`,`Use HNO₃ to acidify, never HCl.`,`State temperature for Cl₂ + NaOH.`,`Know ALL observation colours.`]}]}],quizzes:[{q:`Group VII elements are called:`,opts:[`Alkali metals`,`Halogens`,`Noble gases`,`Transition metals`],ans:1,hint:`Halogen = salt-former.`},{q:`The colour of Br₂ at room temperature is:`,opts:[`Pale yellow`,`Yellow-green`,`Red-brown`,`Grey-black`],ans:2,hint:`Bromine is a distinctive red-brown fuming liquid.`},{q:`I₂ at room temperature is a:`,opts:[`Gas`,`Liquid`,`Solid`,`Plasma`],ans:2,hint:`Iodine is a grey-black solid with a shiny lustre.`},{q:`Volatility of halogens DECREASES down the group because:`,opts:[`Bond energy increases`,`Molecular size increases → stronger London forces`,`They become more ionic`,`They react with water`],ans:1,hint:`More electrons → stronger temporary dipoles → stronger attraction.`},{q:`The most powerful oxidising agent among the halogens is:`,opts:[`Cl₂`,`Br₂`,`I₂`,`F₂`],ans:3,hint:`F₂ has the highest E° value (+2.87 V).`},{q:`Cl₂ can displace Br⁻ from KBr because:`,opts:[`Cl₂ is less reactive`,`Cl₂ is a stronger oxidising agent than Br₂`,`Br₂ is more reactive`,`KBr is insoluble`],ans:1,hint:`Higher E° → stronger oxidiser → displaces lower.`},{q:`When Cl₂ is added to KI solution, the observation is:`,opts:[`No change`,`Solution turns brown/black`,`White precipitate`,`Gas evolved`],ans:1,hint:`I₂ is produced (brown). With starch indicator: blue-black.`},{q:`The thermal stability of hydrogen halides DECREASES because:`,opts:[`The H–X bond gets longer and weaker`,`The H–X bond gets shorter`,`The molecules get smaller`,`More electrons are present`],ans:0,hint:`Longer bond → weaker → easier to break thermally.`},{q:`HI decomposes more easily than HCl because:`,opts:[`HI has a stronger bond`,`H–I bond is longer and weaker than H–Cl`,`I is more electronegative`,`HI is more acidic`],ans:1,hint:`Bond energy: H–I (298) < H–Cl (432) kJ mol⁻¹.`},{q:`To test for Cl⁻ ions, add:`,opts:[`BaCl₂ then HCl`,`AgNO₃ after acidifying with HNO₃`,`NaOH then heat`,`AgNO₃ after acidifying with HCl`],ans:1,hint:`HNO₃ + AgNO₃. White ppt = AgCl.`},{q:`AgCl is what colour?`,opts:[`Yellow`,`Cream`,`White`,`Brown`],ans:2,hint:`White precipitate, soluble in dilute NH₃.`},{q:`AgBr is what colour?`,opts:[`White`,`Cream`,`Yellow`,`Orange`],ans:1,hint:`Cream — between white (AgCl) and yellow (AgI).`},{q:`AgI dissolves in:`,opts:[`Dilute NH₃`,`Concentrated NH₃`,`Neither`,`Both`],ans:2,hint:`AgI is insoluble in both dilute and concentrated ammonia.`},{q:`NaCl + conc. H₂SO₄ produces:`,opts:[`Cl₂ + SO₂`,`HCl only`,`Br₂ + SO₂`,`I₂ + H₂S`],ans:1,hint:`HCl is too weak a reducer to reduce H₂SO₄.`},{q:`NaBr + conc. H₂SO₄ produces:`,opts:[`HBr only`,`HBr then Br₂ and SO₂`,`NaCl + Br₂`,`No reaction`],ans:1,hint:`HBr reduces H₂SO₄ to SO₂.`},{q:`NaI + conc. H₂SO₄ produces:`,opts:[`HI only`,`I₂ + SO₂ only`,`I₂ + H₂S (and SO₂, S)`,`No reaction`],ans:2,hint:`I⁻ reduces H₂SO₄ all the way to H₂S.`},{q:`The reducing power of halide ions INCREASES because:`,opts:[`Ionic radius decreases`,`Ionic radius increases → electrons held less tightly`,`Nuclear charge increases`,`Electronegativity increases`],ans:1,hint:`Larger ion → outer electron easier to remove → better reducer.`},{q:`Disproportionation means:`,opts:[`Same element oxidised AND reduced simultaneously`,`Two different elements react`,`A compound decomposes`,`An acid reacts with a base`],ans:0,hint:`The same element goes to both a higher and lower oxidation state.`},{q:`Cl₂ + cold NaOH produces:`,opts:[`NaCl + NaClO₃`,`NaCl + NaClO`,`NaCl + HClO₃`,`NaCl only`],ans:1,hint:`Cold → NaClO (bleach). Cl: 0 → −1 and +1.`},{q:`Cl₂ + hot NaOH produces:`,opts:[`NaCl + NaClO`,`NaCl + NaClO₃`,`NaCl + HCl`,`NaCl + Cl₂O`],ans:1,hint:`Hot → NaClO₃ (chlorate). Cl: 0 → −1 and +5.`},{q:`The oxidation state of Cl in NaClO is:`,opts:[`−1`,`0`,`+1`,`+5`],ans:2,hint:`Na is +1, O is −2. Na(+1) + Cl + O(−2) = 0. Cl = +1.`},{q:`The oxidation state of Cl in NaClO₃ is:`,opts:[`+1`,`+3`,`+5`,`+7`],ans:2,hint:`Na(+1) + Cl + 3O(−2) = 0. Cl = +5.`},{q:`Chlorine is used for water purification because:`,opts:[`It is colourless`,`HClO kills bacteria by oxidation`,`It makes water taste better`,`It removes calcium`],ans:1,hint:`Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO. HClO is the active disinfectant.`},{q:`CFCs damage the ozone layer by:`,opts:[`Absorbing UV directly`,`Releasing Cl radicals that catalytically destroy O₃`,`Reacting with N₂`,`Absorbing heat`],ans:1,hint:`Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂; one Cl• destroys thousands of O₃.`},{q:`PVC is made from:`,opts:[`Polyethene + Cl₂`,`Chloroethene (vinyl chloride) by polymerisation`,`CCl₄ + ethene`,`Chlorine + sodium`],ans:1,hint:`CH₂=CHCl → (−CH₂CHCl−)ₙ = PVC.`},{q:`The colour of I₂ vapour is:`,opts:[`Brown`,`Purple`,`White`,`Green`],ans:1,hint:`Iodine sublimes to give a characteristic purple gas.`},{q:`In the reaction Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂, chlorine is:`,opts:[`Oxidised`,`Reduced`,`Neither`,`Both`],ans:1,hint:`Cl₂ (0) → Cl⁻ (−1). It gains electrons = reduced.`},{q:`In the same reaction, Br⁻ is:`,opts:[`Oxidised`,`Reduced`,`Neither`,`Both`],ans:0,hint:`Br⁻ (−1) → Br₂ (0). It loses electrons = oxidised.`},{q:`Which halide test precipitate is soluble in dilute NH₃?`,opts:[`AgCl`,`AgBr`,`AgI`,`All of them`],ans:0,hint:`Only AgCl dissolves in dilute ammonia.`},{q:`The H–F bond energy (kJ mol⁻¹) is approximately:`,opts:[`298`,`366`,`432`,`568`],ans:3,hint:`HF has the shortest, strongest H–X bond.`},{q:`Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO. The Cl₂ is:`,opts:[`Only oxidised`,`Only reduced`,`Disproportionated`,`Unchanged`],ans:2,hint:`Cl goes from 0 to both −1 and +1 simultaneously.`},{q:`Tincture of iodine is used as:`,opts:[`A fuel`,`An antiseptic`,`A bleach`,`A food preservative`],ans:1,hint:`I₂ dissolved in ethanol — applied to wounds.`}],multi:[],structured:[{title:`Physical properties and displacement`,marks:8,q:`Consider the halogens Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Describe and explain the trend in boiling point from Cl₂ to I₂.`,marks:3,ans:`Boiling point increases: Cl₂ (−34 °C) < Br₂ (+59 °C) < I₂ (+184 °C). All are simple molecular (X₂) held by van der Waals (London) forces. Going down: the number of electrons per molecule increases (Cl₂ = 34, Br₂ = 70, I₂ = 106 electrons). More electrons → larger, more polarisable electron cloud → stronger instantaneous dipole-induced dipole forces → higher b.p.`},{q:`(b) Write the equation for the reaction of Cl₂ with KI(aq) and state the colour change.`,marks:2,ans:`Cl₂(aq) + 2KI(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + I₂(aq). The colourless/pale green solution turns BROWN as iodine is produced. (With starch indicator: blue-black.)`},{q:`(c) Explain, using E° values, why Cl₂ can displace I⁻ but I₂ cannot displace Cl⁻.`,marks:3,ans:`E°(Cl₂/2Cl⁻) = +1.36 V; E°(I₂/2I⁻) = +0.54 V. For Cl₂ + 2I⁻: E°cell = 1.36 − 0.54 = +0.82 V > 0 → feasible. Cl₂ is a stronger oxidising agent so it can take electrons from I⁻. For I₂ + 2Cl⁻: E°cell = 0.54 − 1.36 = −0.82 V < 0 → not feasible. I₂ is too weak an oxidising agent to remove electrons from Cl⁻.`}]},{title:`Halide tests`,marks:8,q:`An unknown solution may contain Cl⁻, Br⁻ or I⁻ ions.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Describe a test to identify which halide ion is present. Include the reagents, procedure, observations and equations.`,marks:5,ans:`Procedure: (1) Add dilute HNO₃ to the solution to remove CO₃²⁻/SO₃²⁻. (2) Add AgNO₃(aq). (3) Observe precipitate: white = Cl⁻ (AgCl), cream = Br⁻ (AgBr), yellow = I⁻ (AgI). Equations: Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s); Ag⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq) → AgBr(s); Ag⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq) → AgI(s). (4) Confirm by adding NH₃: AgCl dissolves in dilute NH₃ (AgCl + 2NH₃ → [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ + Cl⁻). AgBr dissolves only in concentrated NH₃. AgI is insoluble in both.`},{q:`(b) Explain why HNO₃ is used to acidify, not HCl.`,marks:2,ans:`HCl would introduce Cl⁻ ions into the solution, which would react with AgNO₃ to give a white precipitate of AgCl regardless of the actual halide present — a false positive for chloride. HNO₃ does not contain any halide ions, so it does not interfere with the test.`},{q:`(c) State the formula of the complex formed when AgCl dissolves in ammonia.`,marks:1,ans:`[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ — the diamminesilver(I) ion.`}]},{title:`Conc. H₂SO₄ reactions`,marks:9,q:`Solid NaCl, NaBr and NaI are each treated separately with concentrated sulphuric acid.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for NaCl + conc. H₂SO₄ and state the observation.`,marks:2,ans:`NaCl(s) + H₂SO₄(l) → NaHSO₄(s) + HCl(g). Observation: steamy white fumes of HCl.`},{q:`(b) Write equations to show the two stages of the reaction of NaBr with conc. H₂SO₄.`,marks:3,ans:`Stage 1 (acid-base): NaBr + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HBr. Stage 2 (redox): 2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O. Overall observations: steamy fumes initially, then brown/orange fumes of Br₂ and pungent smell of SO₂.`},{q:`(c) Explain why HBr reduces H₂SO₄ but HCl does not.`,marks:2,ans:`Br⁻ is a stronger reducing agent than Cl⁻. Br⁻ has a larger ionic radius → the outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded → easier to remove. Br⁻ can lose an electron to reduce H₂SO₄ (S: +6→+4 in SO₂). Cl⁻ is a weaker reducer — it cannot provide enough reducing power to reduce H₂SO₄.`},{q:`(d) Write the equation for the reduction of H₂SO₄ by HI to form H₂S, and state the observations.`,marks:2,ans:`8HI + H₂SO₄ → 4I₂ + H₂S + 4H₂O. Observations: purple/black solid or vapour of I₂ and a rotten-egg smell from H₂S gas. Sulphur (yellow solid) may also be deposited as an intermediate reduction product.`}]},{title:`Disproportionation`,marks:8,q:`Chlorine reacts with aqueous sodium hydroxide.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for the reaction of Cl₂ with cold dilute NaOH.`,marks:1,ans:`Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O.`},{q:`(b) State the oxidation states of chlorine in each chlorine-containing product.`,marks:2,ans:`In NaCl: Cl is −1. In NaClO: Cl is +1.`},{q:`(c) Explain why this reaction is described as disproportionation.`,marks:2,ans:`Chlorine in Cl₂ has oxidation state 0. In the products, chlorine has been simultaneously REDUCED to −1 (in NaCl) and OXIDISED to +1 (in NaClO). The same element undergoes both oxidation and reduction — this defines a disproportionation reaction.`},{q:`(d) Write the equation for Cl₂ with hot concentrated NaOH and state the oxidation states.`,marks:2,ans:`3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O. Cl in NaCl: −1. Cl in NaClO₃: +5. Disproportionation: 0 → −1 and +5.`},{q:`(e) State one important use of the product NaClO.`,marks:1,ans:`NaClO (sodium hypochlorite) is used as bleach — for whitening clothes, disinfecting surfaces, and water treatment.`}]},{title:`Hydrogen halides`,marks:7,q:`The hydrogen halides HCl, HBr and HI have different thermal stabilities.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Arrange HCl, HBr and HI in order of decreasing thermal stability.`,marks:1,ans:`HCl > HBr > HI (most stable first).`},{q:`(b) Explain this trend in terms of bond energies.`,marks:3,ans:`Going from HCl to HI, the H–X bond length increases because the halogen atom is larger. Longer bonds are weaker: H–Cl (432 kJ mol⁻¹) > H–Br (366) > H–I (298). Weaker bonds require less energy to break, so HI decomposes at a lower temperature than HCl. HCl is thermally stable because its shorter, stronger bond resists breaking.`},{q:`(c) Describe the reaction of H₂ with I₂ and contrast it with the reaction of H₂ with F₂.`,marks:3,ans:`H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI: slow reaction, requires a catalyst (Pt) and heat. The reaction is REVERSIBLE. H₂ + F₂ → 2HF: explosively fast, even at low temperatures and in the dark. Not reversible under normal conditions. The difference is because the H–F bond (568 kJ mol⁻¹) is much stronger than H–I (298 kJ mol⁻¹), so the HF reaction is much more exothermic and proceeds vigorously.`}]},{title:`Environmental significance`,marks:7,q:`Chlorine compounds have both beneficial and harmful effects on the environment.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Explain why chlorine is added to drinking water.`,marks:2,ans:`Chlorine reacts with water to form HClO (chloric(I) acid): Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO. HClO is a powerful oxidising agent that kills bacteria and other pathogens, making the water safe to drink. This process is called chlorination.`},{q:`(b) Describe how CFCs damage the ozone layer.`,marks:3,ans:`CFCs (e.g. CFCl₃) are very stable and persist in the atmosphere. They rise to the stratosphere where UV radiation breaks the C–Cl bond, releasing chlorine radicals: CFCl₃ → CFCl₂• + Cl•. The Cl radical catalytically destroys ozone: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂; ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂. The Cl radical is regenerated, so one radical can destroy thousands of O₃ molecules before being removed.`},{q:`(c) Explain one environmental concern about halogenated hydrocarbons used as solvents.`,marks:2,ans:`Halogenated solvents such as CCl₄ and CHCl₃ can contaminate groundwater because they are denser than water and sink through soil. They are toxic to aquatic life and potentially carcinogenic to humans. They are also very persistent — their strong C–Cl bonds make them resistant to natural degradation.`}]}],worked:[{title:`Predict displacement reaction`,diff:`easy`,q:`Will Br₂ displace Cl⁻ from NaCl solution? Use E° values: Br₂/2Br⁻ = +1.07 V, Cl₂/2Cl⁻ = +1.36 V.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`For Br₂ to displace Cl⁻: Br₂ must oxidise Cl⁻ to Cl₂.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`E°cell = E°(oxidiser) − E°(species being oxidised) = 1.07 − 1.36 = −0.29 V.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Negative E°cell → reaction is NOT feasible.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Br₂ is a weaker oxidising agent than Cl₂, so it cannot displace Cl⁻.`}],ans:`No. E°cell = −0.29 V < 0. Br₂ cannot displace Cl⁻.`},{title:`Identify halide by Ag⁺ test`,diff:`easy`,q:`An unknown halide gives a cream precipitate with AgNO₃. The precipitate does not dissolve in dilute NH₃ but dissolves in concentrated NH₃. Identify the halide.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Cream precipitate = AgBr (not white = AgCl, not yellow = AgI).`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Does not dissolve in dilute NH₃ — confirms it is NOT AgCl.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Dissolves in concentrated NH₃ — confirms it is AgBr (AgI would not dissolve in either).`}],ans:`The halide is Br⁻ (bromide).`},{title:`NaI + conc. H₂SO₄`,diff:`hard`,q:`Explain fully what happens when NaI(s) is treated with conc. H₂SO₄. Give equations and observations.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Stage 1 (acid-base): NaI + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HI. Steamy fumes initially.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Stage 2 (redox): HI is a very strong reducing agent. It reduces H₂SO₄ through several stages.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`H₂SO₄ reduced to SO₂ (S: +6→+4): 2HI + H₂SO₄ → I₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Further: 6HI + H₂SO₄ → 3I₂ + S + 4H₂O (S: +6→0). And: 8HI + H₂SO₄ → 4I₂ + H₂S + 4H₂O (S: +6→−2).`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Observations: purple/black solid (I₂), rotten egg smell (H₂S), yellow solid (S), pungent smell (SO₂).`}],ans:`Multiple reduction products: SO₂, S, H₂S because I⁻ is a very strong reducing agent. Purple I₂ and rotten-egg H₂S are the key observations.`},{title:`Disproportionation oxidation states`,diff:`medium`,q:`In the reaction Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O, identify the oxidation state changes.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Cl in Cl₂ = 0.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Cl in NaCl: Na is +1, so Cl = −1. This Cl has been REDUCED (0 → −1).`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Cl in NaClO: Na is +1, O is −2. Na(+1) + Cl + O(−2) = 0, so Cl = +1. This Cl has been OXIDISED (0 → +1).`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Same element (Cl) simultaneously oxidised and reduced = disproportionation.`}],ans:`Cl: 0 → −1 (reduced) and 0 → +1 (oxidised). This is disproportionation.`},{title:`Explain reducing power trend`,diff:`medium`,q:`Explain why I⁻ is a stronger reducing agent than Cl⁻.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`I⁻ has a much larger ionic radius than Cl⁻ (220 pm vs 181 pm).`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`The outer electron in I⁻ is further from the nucleus and more shielded by inner shells.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`It is therefore easier to remove this electron from I⁻ (lower IE for I⁻ → I + e⁻).`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Easier electron loss = better reducing agent. So I⁻ > Br⁻ > Cl⁻ in reducing power.`}],ans:`I⁻ is larger → outer electron is further from nucleus and more shielded → easier to lose → stronger reducing agent.`},{title:`Thermal stability of HBr`,diff:`medium`,q:`Explain why HBr is less thermally stable than HCl.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`H–Br bond length is longer than H–Cl (because Br is larger than Cl).`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Longer bond = less effective orbital overlap = weaker bond.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Bond energy: H–Br = 366 kJ mol⁻¹ < H–Cl = 432 kJ mol⁻¹.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Less energy needed to break the weaker H–Br bond → HBr decomposes at a lower temperature.`}],ans:`H–Br bond is longer and weaker (366 vs 432 kJ mol⁻¹) → easier to break → less thermally stable.`},{title:`CFCs and ozone`,diff:`hard`,q:`Explain the mechanism by which CFCs destroy the ozone layer.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`CFCs (e.g. CF₂Cl₂) are very stable in the lower atmosphere — C–Cl bonds are strong enough to resist normal degradation.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`They rise to the stratosphere where high-energy UV radiation has enough energy to break the C–Cl bond (homolytic fission): CF₂Cl₂ → CF₂Cl• + Cl•.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Initiation: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂. The ozone molecule is destroyed.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Propagation: ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂. The Cl• is REGENERATED — it acts as a catalyst.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`One Cl radical can destroy tens of thousands of O₃ molecules before being removed from the cycle.`}],ans:`UV breaks C–Cl bond. Released Cl• catalytically destroys O₃: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂; ClO• + O₃ → Cl• + 2O₂. Cl• is regenerated each cycle.`},{title:`Write hot NaOH equation`,diff:`medium`,q:`Write the balanced equation for chlorine with hot concentrated NaOH. State oxidation state changes.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Cl₂: Cl = 0. NaCl: Cl = −1. NaClO₃: Cl = +5.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Reduction: 0 → −1 (5 Cl atoms). Oxidation: 0 → +5 (1 Cl atom).`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Electrons: 5 × 1e⁻ gained = 5e⁻. 1 × 5e⁻ lost = 5e⁻. Balanced.`}],ans:`3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O. Cl: 0 → −1 (×5) and 0 → +5 (×1).`}],tutorPrompts:[`Walk me through the silver halide test step by step`,`Explain why NaI + conc. H₂SO₄ gives so many products`,`What is disproportionation? Show me both the cold and hot NaOH reactions`,`Give me a hard question about CFCs and the ozone layer mechanism`,`Why can Cl₂ displace Br⁻ but not vice versa? Use E° values.`]};export{e as GROUP7};